{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-163206" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the function $f(x,y)$ and the region $D$ given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=2x+2y,\\,D={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 2}.\n$$\n\nAccording to the mean value theorem for double integrals, there exists a point $(a,b)\\in D$ such that the volume of the solid enclosed between the surface $z=f(x,y)$ and the $xy$-plane over the region $D$ equals the volume of the right cylinder with base $D$ and height $f(a,b).$ Find $a$ given that $b=2.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081614/state.json" "file_sha256" "d5312d2aa5d4b7e1b0790224f6ba9fee205e36425db56a3d3477c09608790ec3"} {"question" "q-163206" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "According to the mean value theorem for double integrals, there exists a point $(a,b)\\in D$ such that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A=f(a,b)\\cdot \\text{Area}(D).\n$$\n\nEvaluating the integral on the left-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{D}(2x+2y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(2x+2y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}[2xy+{y}^{2}]_{y=0}^{y=2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(4x+4)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =[2{x}^{2}+4x]_{x=0}^{x=2} \\\\  & =(2\\cdot {2}^{2}+4\\cdot 2)-0 \\\\  & =16.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(D)=2\\cdot 2=4.\n$$\n\nAlso, we are told that $b=2.$ So, our initial equation simplifies to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}16 & =f(a,2)\\cdot 4 \\\\ 16 & =(2a+2\\cdot 2)\\cdot 4 \\\\ 4 & =2a+4 \\\\ 0 & =2a \\\\ a & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $(0,2)\\in D.$ Therefore, the answer is $a=0.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081614/state.json" "file_sha256" "d5312d2aa5d4b7e1b0790224f6ba9fee205e36425db56a3d3477c09608790ec3"} {"question" "q-163206" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081614/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e8becac77a69f21379018dd4070477e9968bff5ba817ecb38f8dbe13c039151d"} {"question" "q-163206" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081614/state.json" "file_sha256" "d5312d2aa5d4b7e1b0790224f6ba9fee205e36425db56a3d3477c09608790ec3"} {"question" "q-163206" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "0"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{3}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{5}{4}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081614/state.json" "file_sha256" "d5312d2aa5d4b7e1b0790224f6ba9fee205e36425db56a3d3477c09608790ec3"} {"question" "q-163206" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081614/state.json" "file_sha256" "d5312d2aa5d4b7e1b0790224f6ba9fee205e36425db56a3d3477c09608790ec3"} {"question" "q-163207" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the function $f(x,y)$ and the region $D$ given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=x+y,\\,D={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 3,\\,0\\le y\\le 3}.\n$$\n\nAccording to the mean value theorem for double integrals, there exists a point $(a,b)\\in D$ such that the volume of the solid enclosed between the surface $z=f(x,y)$ and the $xy$-plane over the region $D$ equals the volume of the right cylinder with base $D$ and height $f(a,b).$ Find $b$ given that $a=2.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081614/state.json" "file_sha256" "d5312d2aa5d4b7e1b0790224f6ba9fee205e36425db56a3d3477c09608790ec3"} {"question" "q-163207" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "According to the mean value theorem for double integrals, there exists a point $(a,b)\\in D$ such that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A=f(a,b)\\cdot \\text{Area}(D).\n$$\n\nEvaluating the integral on the left-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{D}(x+y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{3}(x+y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}[xy+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]_{y=0}^{y=3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}(3x+\\frac{9}{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =[\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{9x}{2}]_{x=0}^{x=3} \\\\  & =(\\frac{3\\cdot (3)^{2}}{2}+\\frac{9\\cdot (3)}{2})-0 \\\\  & =27.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(D)=3\\cdot 3=9.\n$$\n\nAlso, we are told that $a=2.$ So, our initial equation simplifies to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}27 & =f(2,b)\\cdot 9 \\\\ 27 & =(2+b)\\cdot 9 \\\\ 27 & =9b+18 \\\\ 9 & =9b \\\\ b & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $(2,1)\\in D.$ Therefore, the answer is $b=1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081614/state.json" "file_sha256" "d5312d2aa5d4b7e1b0790224f6ba9fee205e36425db56a3d3477c09608790ec3"} {"question" "q-163207" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081614/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e8becac77a69f21379018dd4070477e9968bff5ba817ecb38f8dbe13c039151d"} {"question" "q-163207" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081614/state.json" "file_sha256" "d5312d2aa5d4b7e1b0790224f6ba9fee205e36425db56a3d3477c09608790ec3"} {"question" "q-163207" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "1"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{4}"] ["math" "\\frac{3}{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081614/state.json" "file_sha256" "d5312d2aa5d4b7e1b0790224f6ba9fee205e36425db56a3d3477c09608790ec3"} {"question" "q-163207" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "1"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081614/state.json" "file_sha256" "d5312d2aa5d4b7e1b0790224f6ba9fee205e36425db56a3d3477c09608790ec3"} {"question" "q-163059" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the function $f(x,y)$ and the region $D$ given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)={x}^{2}+2y+1,\\,D={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 3}.\n$$\n\nAccording to the mean value theorem for double integrals, there exists a point $(a,b)\\in D$ such that the volume of the solid enclosed between the surface $z=f(x,y)$ and the $xy$-plane over the region $D$ equals the volume of the right cylinder with base $D$ and height $f(a,b).$ Find $b$ given that $a=1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081614/state.json" "file_sha256" "d5312d2aa5d4b7e1b0790224f6ba9fee205e36425db56a3d3477c09608790ec3"} {"question" "q-163059" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "According to the mean value theorem for double integrals, there exists a point $(a,b)\\in D$ such that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A=f(a,b)\\cdot \\text{Area}(D).\n$$\n\nEvaluating the integral on the left-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+2y+1)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{3}({x}^{2}+2y+1)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}[{x}^{2}y+{y}^{2}+y]_{y=0}^{y=3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(3{x}^{2}+12)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =[{x}^{3}+12x]_{x=0}^{x=2} \\\\  & =({2}^{3}+12\\cdot 2)-0 \\\\  & =32.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(D)=2\\cdot 3=6.\n$$\n\nAlso, we are told that $a=1.$ So, our initial equation simplifies to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}32 & =f(1,b)\\cdot 6 \\\\ 32 & =({1}^{2}+2b+1)\\cdot 6 \\\\ 32 & =12b+12 \\\\ 20 & =12b \\\\ b & =\\frac{5}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $(1,\\frac{5}{3})\\in D.$ Therefore, the answer is $b=\\frac{5}{3}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081614/state.json" "file_sha256" "d5312d2aa5d4b7e1b0790224f6ba9fee205e36425db56a3d3477c09608790ec3"} {"question" "q-163059" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081614/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e8becac77a69f21379018dd4070477e9968bff5ba817ecb38f8dbe13c039151d"} {"question" "q-163059" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081614/state.json" "file_sha256" "d5312d2aa5d4b7e1b0790224f6ba9fee205e36425db56a3d3477c09608790ec3"} {"question" "q-163059" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "1"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "\\frac{5}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{5}{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{7}{3}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081614/state.json" "file_sha256" "d5312d2aa5d4b7e1b0790224f6ba9fee205e36425db56a3d3477c09608790ec3"} {"question" "q-163059" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{5}{3}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081614/state.json" "file_sha256" "d5312d2aa5d4b7e1b0790224f6ba9fee205e36425db56a3d3477c09608790ec3"} {"question" "q-163153" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the function $f(x,y)$ and the region $D$ given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=2x+4y,\\,D={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 2x}.\n$$\n\nAccording to the mean value theorem for double integrals, there exists a point $(a,b)\\in D$ such that the volume of the solid enclosed between the surface $z=f(x,y)$ and the $xy$-plane over the region $D$ equals the volume of the right cylinder with base $D$ and height $f(a,b).$ Find $a$ given that $b=1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081614/state.json" "file_sha256" "d5312d2aa5d4b7e1b0790224f6ba9fee205e36425db56a3d3477c09608790ec3"} {"question" "q-163153" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "According to the mean value theorem for double integrals, there exists a point $(a,b)\\in D$ such that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A=f(a,b)\\cdot \\text{Area}(D).\n$$\n\nEvaluating the integral on the left-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{D}(2x+4y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2x}(2x+4y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}[2xy+2{y}^{2}]_{y=0}^{y=2x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}12{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =[4{x}^{3}]_{x=0}^{x=2} \\\\  & =32.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2x}\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}[y]_{y=0}^{y=2x}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}2x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[{x}^{2}]}_{x=0}^{x=2} \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, we are told that $b=1.$ So, our initial equation simplifies to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}32 & =f(a,1)\\cdot 4 \\\\ 32 & =(2a+4\\cdot 1)\\cdot 4 \\\\ 8 & =2a+4 \\\\ 4 & =2a \\\\ a & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $(2,1)\\in D.$ Therefore, the answer is $a=2.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081614/state.json" "file_sha256" "d5312d2aa5d4b7e1b0790224f6ba9fee205e36425db56a3d3477c09608790ec3"} {"question" "q-163153" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081614/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e8becac77a69f21379018dd4070477e9968bff5ba817ecb38f8dbe13c039151d"} {"question" "q-163153" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081614/state.json" "file_sha256" "d5312d2aa5d4b7e1b0790224f6ba9fee205e36425db56a3d3477c09608790ec3"} {"question" "q-163153" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "1"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{3}{4}"] ["math" "\\frac{5}{4}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081614/state.json" "file_sha256" "d5312d2aa5d4b7e1b0790224f6ba9fee205e36425db56a3d3477c09608790ec3"} {"question" "q-163153" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081614/state.json" "file_sha256" "d5312d2aa5d4b7e1b0790224f6ba9fee205e36425db56a3d3477c09608790ec3"} {"question" "q-163148" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the function $f(x,y)$ and the region $D$ given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=6{x}^{2}-2y+2,\\,D={(x,y)\\,:\\,-1\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 1}.\n$$\n\nAccording to the mean value theorem for double integrals, there exists a point $(a,b)\\in D$ such that the volume of the solid enclosed between the surface $z=f(x,y)$ and the $xy$-plane over the region $D$ equals the volume of the right cylinder with base $D$ and height $f(a,b).$ Find $b$ given that $a=\\frac{1}{2}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081614/state.json" "file_sha256" "d5312d2aa5d4b7e1b0790224f6ba9fee205e36425db56a3d3477c09608790ec3"} {"question" "q-163148" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "According to the mean value theorem for double integrals, there exists a point $(a,b)\\in D$ such that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A=f(a,b)\\cdot \\text{Area}(D).\n$$\n\nEvaluating the integral on the left-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{D}(6{x}^{2}-2y+2)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(6{x}^{2}-2y+2)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}[6{x}^{2}y-{y}^{2}+2y]_{y=0}^{y=1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}(6{x}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =[2{x}^{3}+x]_{x=-1}^{x=1} \\\\  & =(2\\cdot {1}^{3}+1)-(2\\cdot (-1)^{3}+(-1)) \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(D)=1\\cdot 2=2.\n$$\n\nAlso, we are told that $a=\\frac{1}{2}.$ So, our initial equation simplifies to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6 & =f(\\frac{1}{2},b)\\cdot 2 \\\\ 6 & =(6\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}-2b+2)\\cdot 2 \\\\ 3 & =\\frac{7}{2}-2b \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2} & =-2b \\\\ b & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{4})\\in D.$ Therefore, the answer is $b=\\frac{1}{4}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081614/state.json" "file_sha256" "d5312d2aa5d4b7e1b0790224f6ba9fee205e36425db56a3d3477c09608790ec3"} {"question" "q-163148" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081614/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e8becac77a69f21379018dd4070477e9968bff5ba817ecb38f8dbe13c039151d"} {"question" "q-163148" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081614/state.json" "file_sha256" "d5312d2aa5d4b7e1b0790224f6ba9fee205e36425db56a3d3477c09608790ec3"} {"question" "q-163148" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-\\frac{2}{3}"] ["math" "1"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{4}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081614/state.json" "file_sha256" "d5312d2aa5d4b7e1b0790224f6ba9fee205e36425db56a3d3477c09608790ec3"} {"question" "q-163148" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{4}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081614/state.json" "file_sha256" "d5312d2aa5d4b7e1b0790224f6ba9fee205e36425db56a3d3477c09608790ec3"} {"question" "q-163246" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the average value of the function $f(x,y)=2x+3{y}^{2}$ on the region $D={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 2}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081614/state.json" "file_sha256" "d5312d2aa5d4b7e1b0790224f6ba9fee205e36425db56a3d3477c09608790ec3"} {"question" "q-163246" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, notice that \n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(D)=1\\cdot 2=2.\n$$\n\nApplying the average value formula, we find that the average value of the function is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{\\text{ave}} & =\\frac{1}{\\text{Area}(D)}\\,{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{∬}_{D}(2x+3{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(2x+3{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}[2xy+{y}^{3}]_{y=0}^{y=2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(4x+8)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[2{x}^{2}+8x]_{x=0}^{x=1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(2\\cdot {1}^{2}+8\\cdot 1) \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{2} \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081614/state.json" "file_sha256" "d5312d2aa5d4b7e1b0790224f6ba9fee205e36425db56a3d3477c09608790ec3"} {"question" "q-163246" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081614/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e8becac77a69f21379018dd4070477e9968bff5ba817ecb38f8dbe13c039151d"} {"question" "q-163246" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081614/state.json" "file_sha256" "d5312d2aa5d4b7e1b0790224f6ba9fee205e36425db56a3d3477c09608790ec3"} {"question" "q-163246" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "3"] ["math" "4"] ["math" "5"] ["math" "6"] ["math" "7"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081614/state.json" "file_sha256" "d5312d2aa5d4b7e1b0790224f6ba9fee205e36425db56a3d3477c09608790ec3"} {"question" "q-163246" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "5"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081614/state.json" "file_sha256" "d5312d2aa5d4b7e1b0790224f6ba9fee205e36425db56a3d3477c09608790ec3"} {"question" "q-163235" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the average value of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{3}+6{y}^{2}$ on the region $D={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 1}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081614/state.json" "file_sha256" "d5312d2aa5d4b7e1b0790224f6ba9fee205e36425db56a3d3477c09608790ec3"} {"question" "q-163235" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, notice that \n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(D)=2\\cdot 1=2.\n$$\n\nApplying the average value formula, we find that the average value of the function is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{\\text{ave}} & =\\frac{1}{\\text{Area}(D)}\\,{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{∬}_{D}({x}^{3}+6{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{3}+6{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}[{x}^{3}y+2{y}^{3}]_{y=0}^{y=1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}({x}^{3}+2)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{[\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}+2x]}_{x=0}^{x=2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{{2}^{4}}{4}+2\\cdot 2-0) \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081614/state.json" "file_sha256" "d5312d2aa5d4b7e1b0790224f6ba9fee205e36425db56a3d3477c09608790ec3"} {"question" "q-163235" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081614/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e8becac77a69f21379018dd4070477e9968bff5ba817ecb38f8dbe13c039151d"} {"question" "q-163235" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081614/state.json" "file_sha256" "d5312d2aa5d4b7e1b0790224f6ba9fee205e36425db56a3d3477c09608790ec3"} {"question" "q-163235" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "4"] ["math" "5"] ["math" "6"] ["math" "\\frac{21}{4}"] ["math" "\\frac{7}{4}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081614/state.json" "file_sha256" "d5312d2aa5d4b7e1b0790224f6ba9fee205e36425db56a3d3477c09608790ec3"} {"question" "q-163235" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "4"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081614/state.json" "file_sha256" "d5312d2aa5d4b7e1b0790224f6ba9fee205e36425db56a3d3477c09608790ec3"} {"question" "q-163241" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the average value of the function $f(x,y)=x+3{y}^{2}$ on the region $D={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,x\\le y\\le x+1}$ given that the area of the region is $2.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081614/state.json" "file_sha256" "d5312d2aa5d4b7e1b0790224f6ba9fee205e36425db56a3d3477c09608790ec3"} {"question" "q-163241" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Applying the average value formula, we find that the average value of the function is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{\\text{ave}} & =\\frac{1}{\\text{Area}(D)}\\,{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{∬}_{D}x+3{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{x}^{x+1}x+3{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}[xy+{y}^{3}]_{y=x}^{y=x+1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}[3{x}^{2}+4x+1]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[{x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}+x]_{x=0}^{x=2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(18-0) \\\\  & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081614/state.json" "file_sha256" "d5312d2aa5d4b7e1b0790224f6ba9fee205e36425db56a3d3477c09608790ec3"} {"question" "q-163241" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081614/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e8becac77a69f21379018dd4070477e9968bff5ba817ecb38f8dbe13c039151d"} {"question" "q-163241" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081614/state.json" "file_sha256" "d5312d2aa5d4b7e1b0790224f6ba9fee205e36425db56a3d3477c09608790ec3"} {"question" "q-163241" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "3"] ["math" "4"] ["math" "5"] ["math" "6"] ["math" "9"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081614/state.json" "file_sha256" "d5312d2aa5d4b7e1b0790224f6ba9fee205e36425db56a3d3477c09608790ec3"} {"question" "q-163241" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "9"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081614/state.json" "file_sha256" "d5312d2aa5d4b7e1b0790224f6ba9fee205e36425db56a3d3477c09608790ec3"} {"question" "q-163255" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the average value of the function $f(x,y)=12x{y}^{3}$ on the region $D={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le \\frac{x}{4}}$ given that the area of the region is $\\frac{1}{2}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081614/state.json" "file_sha256" "d5312d2aa5d4b7e1b0790224f6ba9fee205e36425db56a3d3477c09608790ec3"} {"question" "q-163255" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Applying the average value formula, we find that the average value of the function is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{\\text{ave}} & =\\frac{1}{\\text{Area}(D)}\\,{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{1}{2})}{∬}_{D}12x{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{x/4}12x{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{2}[3x{y}^{4}]_{y=0}^{y=x/4}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{3{x}^{5}}{256}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2[\\frac{{x}^{6}}{512}]_{x=0}^{x=2} \\\\  & =2(\\frac{1}{8}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081614/state.json" "file_sha256" "d5312d2aa5d4b7e1b0790224f6ba9fee205e36425db56a3d3477c09608790ec3"} {"question" "q-163255" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081614/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e8becac77a69f21379018dd4070477e9968bff5ba817ecb38f8dbe13c039151d"} {"question" "q-163255" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081614/state.json" "file_sha256" "d5312d2aa5d4b7e1b0790224f6ba9fee205e36425db56a3d3477c09608790ec3"} {"question" "q-163255" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "1"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{4}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{8}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081614/state.json" "file_sha256" "d5312d2aa5d4b7e1b0790224f6ba9fee205e36425db56a3d3477c09608790ec3"} {"question" "q-163255" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{4}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081614/state.json" "file_sha256" "d5312d2aa5d4b7e1b0790224f6ba9fee205e36425db56a3d3477c09608790ec3"} {"question" "q-163063" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the average value of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}-4{y}^{3}$ on the region $D={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 3}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081614/state.json" "file_sha256" "d5312d2aa5d4b7e1b0790224f6ba9fee205e36425db56a3d3477c09608790ec3"} {"question" "q-163063" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, notice that \n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(D)=1\\cdot 3=3.\n$$\n\nApplying the average value formula, we find that the average value of the function is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{\\text{ave}} & =\\frac{1}{\\text{Area}(D)}\\,{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{∬}_{D}({x}^{2}-4{y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{3}({x}^{2}-4{y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}[{x}^{2}y-{y}^{4}]_{y=0}^{y=3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(3{x}^{2}-81)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[{x}^{3}-81x]_{x=0}^{x=1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}({1}^{3}-81\\cdot 1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{80}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081614/state.json" "file_sha256" "d5312d2aa5d4b7e1b0790224f6ba9fee205e36425db56a3d3477c09608790ec3"} {"question" "q-163063" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081614/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e8becac77a69f21379018dd4070477e9968bff5ba817ecb38f8dbe13c039151d"} {"question" "q-163063" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081614/state.json" "file_sha256" "d5312d2aa5d4b7e1b0790224f6ba9fee205e36425db56a3d3477c09608790ec3"} {"question" "q-163063" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-12"] ["math" "-\\frac{42}{3}"] ["math" "-\\frac{80}{3}"] ["math" "24"] ["math" "5"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081614/state.json" "file_sha256" "d5312d2aa5d4b7e1b0790224f6ba9fee205e36425db56a3d3477c09608790ec3"} {"question" "q-163063" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\frac{80}{3}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081614/state.json" "file_sha256" "d5312d2aa5d4b7e1b0790224f6ba9fee205e36425db56a3d3477c09608790ec3"} {"question" "q-163261" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the average value of the function $f(x,y)=x-y$ on the portion of the disk of radius $1$ centered at the origin, where $y\\le 0.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081614/state.json" "file_sha256" "d5312d2aa5d4b7e1b0790224f6ba9fee205e36425db56a3d3477c09608790ec3"} {"question" "q-163261" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, let's sketch our region $D:$ \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081614/assets/95faae88fb4909a86492e45101419fe872aeb1cc2eeabde9cf773c8f71d31fff.png)\n\nNotice that \n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(D)=\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\pi \\cdot {1}^{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nThe average value of the function over a region $D$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{ave}}=\\frac{1}{\\text{Area}(D)}\\,{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nIn polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 1,\\,\\pi \\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi }.\n$$\n\nUsing the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}x-y\\,dA & ={∬}_{Δ}(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}{r}^{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ){\\int }_{0}^{1}{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\frac{1}{3}{[{r}^{3}]}_{0}^{1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )({1}^{3}-{0}^{3})\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ]_{\\pi }^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\pi +\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\pi )-(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi )] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(1-(-1)) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{ave}}=\\frac{2}{\\pi }\\cdot \\frac{2}{3}=\\frac{4}{3\\pi }.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081614/state.json" "file_sha256" "d5312d2aa5d4b7e1b0790224f6ba9fee205e36425db56a3d3477c09608790ec3"} {"question" "q-163261" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081614/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e8becac77a69f21379018dd4070477e9968bff5ba817ecb38f8dbe13c039151d"} {"question" "q-163261" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081614/state.json" "file_sha256" "d5312d2aa5d4b7e1b0790224f6ba9fee205e36425db56a3d3477c09608790ec3"} {"question" "q-163261" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{1}{3\\pi }"] ["math" "\\frac{2}{3\\pi }"] ["math" "\\frac{4}{3\\pi }"] ["math" "\\frac{4}{\\pi }"] ["math" "\\frac{8}{\\pi }"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081614/state.json" "file_sha256" "d5312d2aa5d4b7e1b0790224f6ba9fee205e36425db56a3d3477c09608790ec3"} {"question" "q-163261" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{4}{3\\pi }"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081614/state.json" "file_sha256" "d5312d2aa5d4b7e1b0790224f6ba9fee205e36425db56a3d3477c09608790ec3"} {"question" "q-163260" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the average value of the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}$ on the portion of the disk of radius $3$ centered at the origin, where $x\\le 0.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081614/state.json" "file_sha256" "d5312d2aa5d4b7e1b0790224f6ba9fee205e36425db56a3d3477c09608790ec3"} {"question" "q-163260" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, let's sketch our region $D:$ \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081614/assets/25c1f00e588ddaa130f854d9f4e9dfe9b1158d7952a01bae8108ea4b94d3c347.png)\n\nNotice that \n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(D)=\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\pi \\cdot {3}^{2}=\\frac{9\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nThe average value of the function over a region $D$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{ave}}=\\frac{1}{\\text{Area}(D)}\\,{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nIn polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 3,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{3\\pi }{2}}.\n$$\n\nUsing the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{2}{(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}+1}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\frac{2r}{{r}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}[\\operatorname{ln}⁡({r}^{2}+1)]_{0}^{3}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡10-\\operatorname{ln}⁡1)\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡10{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡10[\\theta ]_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡10(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}-\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =\\pi \\operatorname{ln}⁡10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{ave}}=\\frac{2}{9\\pi }\\cdot \\pi \\operatorname{ln}⁡10=\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡10}{9}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081614/state.json" "file_sha256" "d5312d2aa5d4b7e1b0790224f6ba9fee205e36425db56a3d3477c09608790ec3"} {"question" "q-163260" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081614/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e8becac77a69f21379018dd4070477e9968bff5ba817ecb38f8dbe13c039151d"} {"question" "q-163260" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081614/state.json" "file_sha256" "d5312d2aa5d4b7e1b0790224f6ba9fee205e36425db56a3d3477c09608790ec3"} {"question" "q-163260" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡10}{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡10}{9}"] ["math" "\\frac{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡10}{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{9}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡10}{9}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081614/state.json" "file_sha256" "d5312d2aa5d4b7e1b0790224f6ba9fee205e36425db56a3d3477c09608790ec3"} {"question" "q-163260" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡10}{9}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081614/state.json" "file_sha256" "d5312d2aa5d4b7e1b0790224f6ba9fee205e36425db56a3d3477c09608790ec3"} {"question" "q-163278" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081614/assets/f84a8fcd4ed668a6b94d5b084ade49dc8fc027feff4baea862e2feaf68221534.png)\n\nFind the average value of the function $f(x,y)=y\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}$ on the semicircle shown above." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081614/state.json" "file_sha256" "d5312d2aa5d4b7e1b0790224f6ba9fee205e36425db56a3d3477c09608790ec3"} {"question" "q-163278" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, notice that \n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(D)=\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\pi \\cdot {1}^{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nThe average value of the function over a region $D$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{ave}}=\\frac{1}{\\text{Area}(D)}\\,{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents a portion of the disk of radius $1$ centered at \n$(-1,0).$\n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the $y=\\sqrt{-{x}^{2}-2x}$ in polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\sqrt{-{x}^{2}-2x} \\\\ {y}^{2} & =-{x}^{2}-2x \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =-2x \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2} & =-2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ) & =-2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ {r}^{2} & =-2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ {r}^{2}+2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ r(r+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire semicircle, we must have $r=-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .$\n\nTherefore, in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le -2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\pi }.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}y\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\sqrt{(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta [\\frac{{r}^{4}}{4}]_{0}^{-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ((-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{4}-{0}^{4})\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }16\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {cos}^{4}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =4{[-\\frac{{cos}^{5}⁡\\theta }{5}]}_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{5}({cos}^{5}⁡\\pi -{cos}^{5}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{ave}}=\\frac{1}{(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}\\cdot \\frac{4}{5}=\\frac{8}{5\\pi }.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081614/state.json" "file_sha256" "d5312d2aa5d4b7e1b0790224f6ba9fee205e36425db56a3d3477c09608790ec3"} {"question" "q-163278" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081614/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e8becac77a69f21379018dd4070477e9968bff5ba817ecb38f8dbe13c039151d"} {"question" "q-163278" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081614/state.json" "file_sha256" "d5312d2aa5d4b7e1b0790224f6ba9fee205e36425db56a3d3477c09608790ec3"} {"question" "q-163278" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{2\\pi }{5}"] ["math" "\\frac{2}{3\\pi }"] ["math" "\\frac{8\\pi }{5}"] ["math" "\\frac{8}{3\\pi }"] ["math" "\\frac{8}{5\\pi }"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081614/state.json" "file_sha256" "d5312d2aa5d4b7e1b0790224f6ba9fee205e36425db56a3d3477c09608790ec3"} {"question" "q-163278" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{8}{5\\pi }"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081614/state.json" "file_sha256" "d5312d2aa5d4b7e1b0790224f6ba9fee205e36425db56a3d3477c09608790ec3"} {"question" "q-163279" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081614/assets/719f86db69489f62bcbe2753e88cdc6713f04dc6f53415cc2484e2eaab8ffac3.png)\n\nFind the average value of the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{x}{y}$ on the semicircle shown above." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081614/state.json" "file_sha256" "d5312d2aa5d4b7e1b0790224f6ba9fee205e36425db56a3d3477c09608790ec3"} {"question" "q-163279" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, notice that \n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(D)=\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\pi \\cdot {1}^{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nThe average value of the function over a region $D$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{ave}}=\\frac{1}{\\text{Area}(D)}\\,{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents a portion of the disk of radius $1$ centered at \n$(0,1).$\n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the $x=\\sqrt{2y-{y}^{2}}$ in polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\sqrt{2y-{y}^{2}} \\\\ {x}^{2} & =2y-{y}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =2y \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2} & =2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ) & =2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ {r}^{2} & =2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ {r}^{2}-2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ r(r-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire semicircle, we must have $r=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .$\n\nTherefore, in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{x}{y}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\int }_{0}^{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{[\\frac{{r}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }(2{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta -0)\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta ]}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi -\\operatorname{cos}⁡0) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}(-1-1) \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{ave}}=\\frac{1}{(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}\\cdot 1=\\frac{2}{\\pi }.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081614/state.json" "file_sha256" "d5312d2aa5d4b7e1b0790224f6ba9fee205e36425db56a3d3477c09608790ec3"} {"question" "q-163279" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081614/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e8becac77a69f21379018dd4070477e9968bff5ba817ecb38f8dbe13c039151d"} {"question" "q-163279" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081614/state.json" "file_sha256" "d5312d2aa5d4b7e1b0790224f6ba9fee205e36425db56a3d3477c09608790ec3"} {"question" "q-163279" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "0"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{\\pi }"] ["math" "\\frac{2}{\\pi }"] ["math" "\\frac{5}{\\pi }"] ["math" "\\frac{\\pi }{4}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081614/state.json" "file_sha256" "d5312d2aa5d4b7e1b0790224f6ba9fee205e36425db56a3d3477c09608790ec3"} {"question" "q-163279" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{2}{\\pi }"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081614/state.json" "file_sha256" "d5312d2aa5d4b7e1b0790224f6ba9fee205e36425db56a3d3477c09608790ec3"} {"question" "q-163070" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081614/assets/fd1a5a140c3a2d430312d6f6ceeb21d6b7282119c8ec53365553ff845d8c64d5.png)\n\nFind the average value of the function $f(x,y)=x\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}$ on the semicircle shown above." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081614/state.json" "file_sha256" "d5312d2aa5d4b7e1b0790224f6ba9fee205e36425db56a3d3477c09608790ec3"} {"question" "q-163070" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, notice that \n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(D)=\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\pi \\cdot (\\frac{1}{2})^{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{8}.\n$$\n\nThe average value of the function over a region $D$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{ave}}=\\frac{1}{\\text{Area}(D)}\\,{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents a portion of the disk of radius $\\frac{1}{2}$ centered at \n$(0,-\\frac{1}{2}).$\n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the $x=\\sqrt{-{y}^{2}-y}$ in polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\sqrt{-{y}^{2}-y} \\\\ {x}^{2} & =-{y}^{2}-y \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =-y \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2} & =-r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ) & =-r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ {r}^{2} & =-r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ {r}^{2}+r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ r(r+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire semicircle, we must have $r=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .$\n\nTherefore, in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le 0}.\n$$\n\nUsing the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}x\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\sqrt{(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta [\\frac{{r}^{4}}{4}]_{0}^{-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ((-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{4}-{0}^{4})\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {sin}^{4}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{[\\frac{{sin}^{5}⁡\\theta }{5}]}_{-\\pi /2}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{20}({sin}^{5}⁡(0)-{sin}^{5}⁡(-\\frac{\\pi }{2})) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{20}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{ave}}=\\frac{1}{(\\frac{\\pi }{8})}\\cdot \\frac{1}{20}=\\frac{2}{5\\pi }.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081614/state.json" "file_sha256" "d5312d2aa5d4b7e1b0790224f6ba9fee205e36425db56a3d3477c09608790ec3"} {"question" "q-163070" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081614/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e8becac77a69f21379018dd4070477e9968bff5ba817ecb38f8dbe13c039151d"} {"question" "q-163070" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081614/state.json" "file_sha256" "d5312d2aa5d4b7e1b0790224f6ba9fee205e36425db56a3d3477c09608790ec3"} {"question" "q-163070" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{2}{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{2}{5\\pi }"] ["math" "\\frac{3}{2\\pi }"] ["math" "\\frac{4}{3\\pi }"] ["math" "\\frac{4}{3}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081614/state.json" "file_sha256" "d5312d2aa5d4b7e1b0790224f6ba9fee205e36425db56a3d3477c09608790ec3"} {"question" "q-163070" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{2}{5\\pi }"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081614/state.json" "file_sha256" "d5312d2aa5d4b7e1b0790224f6ba9fee205e36425db56a3d3477c09608790ec3"} {"question" "e-12490" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the function $f(x,y)$ and the region $D$ given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y) =\\dfrac{xy}2, \\qquad\nD = \\big\\{ (x,y) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq x \\leq 2y, \\: 0 \\leq y \\leq 4 \\big\\}.\n$$\n\nAccording to the mean value theorem for double integrals, there exists a point $(a,b) \\in D$ such that the volume of the solid enclosed between the surface $z=f(x,y)$ and the $xy$-plane over the region $D$ equals the volume of the right cylinder with base $D$ and height $f(a,b).$ Find $a$ given that $b=2.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081614/example-12490.json" "file_sha256" "140298b749f4f5f77a53f68eff8bbd604c05984f90fd6642a1a7fd303e874b69"} {"question" "e-12490" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "According to the mean value theorem for double integrals, there exists a point $(a,b) \\in D$ such that\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_D f(x,y) \\: \\textrm{d}A = f(a,b) \\cdot \\textrm{Area}(D).\n$$\n\nEvaluating the integral on the left-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{xy}{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{0}^{2y}\\frac{xy}{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}[\\frac{{x}^{2}y}{4}]_{x=0}^{x=2y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =[\\frac{{y}^{4}}{4}]_{y=0}^{y=4} \\\\  & =64.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{0}^{2y}\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}[x]_{x=0}^{x=2y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}2y\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[{y}^{2}]}_{y=0}^{y=4} \\\\  & =16.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, we are told that $b=2.$ So, our initial equation simplifies to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}64 & =f(a,2)\\cdot 16 \\\\ 64 & =\\frac{a\\cdot 2}{2}\\cdot 16 \\\\ 64 & =16a \\\\ a & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $\\left( 4,2 \\right) \\in D.$ Therefore, the answer is $a = 4.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081614/example-12490.json" "file_sha256" "140298b749f4f5f77a53f68eff8bbd604c05984f90fd6642a1a7fd303e874b69"} {"question" "e-12491" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the average value of the function $f(x,y) = xy$ on the region $D = \\big\\{ (x,y) \\: : \\: -1 \\leq x \\leq 2, \\: x \\leq y \\leq 3x \\big\\}$ given that the area of the region is $3$ square units." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081614/example-12491.json" "file_sha256" "f62441933a23d2c3a250758c0e050c21cf4e65596118244b8f5f8f88c7ea0bc7"} {"question" "e-12491" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Applying the average value formula, we find that the average value of the function is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{\\text{ave}} & =\\frac{1}{\\text{Area}(D)}\\,{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{∬}_{D}xy\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{-1}^{2}{\\int }_{x}^{3x}xy\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{-1}^{2}[\\frac{x{y}^{2}}{2}]_{y=x}^{y=3x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{-1}^{2}4{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[{x}^{4}]_{x=-1}^{x=2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(16-1) \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081614/example-12491.json" "file_sha256" "f62441933a23d2c3a250758c0e050c21cf4e65596118244b8f5f8f88c7ea0bc7"} {"question" "e-12492" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the average value of the function $f(x,y) = 15xy^2$ on the portion of the disk of radius $1$ centered at the origin, where $x\\geq 0,$ and $y \\leq 0.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081614/example-12492.json" "file_sha256" "9b5cedeed84cc91ebca682cf0801e9d42fdf65ae8bfd548d28f426ba1bcd80b6"} {"question" "e-12492" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, let's sketch our region $D\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081614/assets/a381fede820f9fc5ba08bb9569906d41a3e8f779de38b446c4f271ceef3a5ccd.png)\n\nNotice that \n\n$$\n\\textrm{Area}(D) = \\dfrac{1}{4} \\cdot \\pi \\cdot 1^2 = \\dfrac\\pi 4.\n$$\n\nThe average value of the function over a region $D$ is given by\n\n$$\nf_{\\textrm{ave}} = \\dfrac{1}{\\textrm{Area}(D)} \\, \\iint\\limits_D f(x,y) \\: \\textrm{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{D} f(x,y) \\ \\mathrm d A = \\iint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\ f(r\\cos\\theta, r\\sin\\theta) \\:  r \\ \\mathrm d r \\mathrm d \\theta.\n$$\n\nIn polar coordinates $(r, \\theta),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\n\\Delta = \\left\\{ (r,\\theta) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq r \\leq 1, \\: \\dfrac{3\\pi}{2} \\leq \\theta \\leq 2\\pi\\right\\}.\n$$\n\nUsing the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}15x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}15r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot (r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}15{r}^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{1}5{r}^{4}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,{[{r}^{5}]}_{0}^{1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ({1}^{5}-0)\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={[{sin}^{3}⁡\\theta ]}_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =({sin}^{3}⁡(2\\pi )-{sin}^{3}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})) \\\\  & =(0-(-1)) \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\nf_{\\textrm{ave}} = \\dfrac{4}{\\pi} \\cdot 1 = \\dfrac{4}{\\pi}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081614/example-12492.json" "file_sha256" "9b5cedeed84cc91ebca682cf0801e9d42fdf65ae8bfd548d28f426ba1bcd80b6"}] "usage" {"e-12492" [] "q-163063" [] "e-12491" [] "q-163059" [] "e-12490" []} "basis" 2842 "retractions" [[17592186167070 :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x, y)$ and the region $D$ given by\n\n$f(x, y) = x^{2} + 2y + 1, D = {(x, y): 0 \\le x \\le 2, 0 \\le y \\le 3}$.\n\nAccording to the mean value theorem for double integrals, there exists a point $(a, b) ∈ D$ such that the volume of the solid enclosed between the surface $z = f(x, y)$ and the $xy$-plane over the region $D$ equals the volume of the right cylinder with base $D$ and height $f(a, b)$. Find $b$ given that $a = 1$."] [17592186167072 :question/problem "Find the average value of the function $f(x, y) = x^{2} - 4y^{3}$ on the region $D = {(x, y): 0 \\le x \\le 1, 0 \\le y \\le 3}$."] [17592186166420 :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x,y)$ and the region $D$ given by\n\n$f(x,y) =\\dfrac{xy}2, \\qquad D = \\big\\{(x,y) \\:: \\: 0 \\leq x \\leq 2y, \\: 0 \\leq y \\leq 4 \\big\\}$.\n\nAccording to the mean value theorem for double integrals, there exists a point $(a,b) \\in D$ such that the volume of the solid enclosed between the surface $z=f(x,y)$ and the $xy$-plane over the region $D$ equals the volume of the right cylinder with base $D$ and height $f(a,b)$. Find $a$ given that $b=2$."] [17592186166420 :question/worked-solution "According to the mean value theorem for double integrals, there exists a point $(a,b) \\in D$ such that\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_D f(x,y) \\: \\textrm{d}A = f(a,b) \\cdot \\textrm{Area}(D)\n$$\n\nEvaluating the integral on the left-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∬_(D)f(x, y)dA &= ∬_(D)\\frac{xy}{2}dA \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{4}∫_{0}^{2y}\\frac{xy}{2}dxdy \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{4}[\\frac{x^{2}y}{4}]_{x = 0}^{x = 2y}dy \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{4}y^{3}dy \\\\\n&= [\\frac{y^{4}}{4}]_{y = 0}^{y = 4} \\\\\n&= 64\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nArea(D) &= ∬_(D)dA \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{4}∫_{0}^{2y}dxdy \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{4}[x]_{x = 0}^{x = 2y}dy \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{4}2ydy \\\\\n&= [y^{2}]_{y = 0}^{y = 4} \\\\\n&= 16\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, we are told that $b=2$. So, our initial equation simplifies to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n64 &= f(a, 2) \\cdot 16 \\\\\n64 &= \\frac{a \\cdot 2}{2} \\cdot 16 \\\\\n64 &= 16a \\\\\na &= 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n\\left(4,2 \\right) \\in D\n$$\n\nTherefore, the answer is $a = 4$."] [17592186166421 :question/problem "Find the average value of the function $f(x,y) = xy$ on the region $D = \\big\\{(x,y) \\:: \\: -1 \\leq x \\leq 2, \\: x \\leq y \\leq 3x \\big\\}$ given that the area of the region is $3$ square units."] [17592186166421 :question/worked-solution "Applying the average value formula, we find that the average value of the function is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_{ave} &= (1)/(Area(D))∬_(D)f(x, y)dA \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3}∬_(D)xydA \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3}∫_{-1}^{2}∫_{x}^{3x}xydydx \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3}∫_{-1}^{2}[\\frac{xy^{2}}{2}]_{y = x}^{y = 3x}dx \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3}∫_{-1}^{2}4x^{3}dx \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3}[x^{4}]_{x =-1}^{x = 2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3}(16 - 1) \\\\\n&= 5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186166422 :question/problem "Find the average value of the function $f(x,y) = 15xy^2$ on the portion of the disk of radius $1$ centered at the origin, where $x\\geq 0$, and $y \\leq 0$."] [17592186166422 :question/worked-solution "First, let's sketch our region $D$:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3147/Source/Images/810ee225582208f31ff49948545b0349.png)\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Area}(D) = \\dfrac{1}{4} \\cdot \\pi \\cdot 1^2 = \\dfrac\\pi 4\n$$\n\nThe average value of the function over a region $D$ is given by\n\n$$\nf_{\\textrm{ave}} = \\dfrac{1}{\\textrm{Area}(D)} \\, \\iint\\limits_D f(x,y) \\: \\textrm{d}A\n$$\n\nWe will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{D} f(x,y) \\ \\mathrm d A = \\iint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\ f(r\\cos\\theta, r\\sin\\theta) \\: r \\ \\mathrm d r \\mathrm d \\theta\n$$\n\nIn polar coordinates $(r, \\theta)$, the region can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\Delta = \\left\\{(r,\\theta) \\:: \\: 0 \\leq r \\leq 1, \\: \\dfrac{3\\pi}{2} \\leq \\theta \\leq 2\\pi\\right\\}\n$$\n\nUsing the change of variables formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∬_(D)15xy^{2}dA &= ∬_(Δ)15r\\cos θ \\cdot (r\\sin θ)^{2}rdrdθ \\\\\n&= ∫_{3π/2}^{2π}∫_{0}^{1}15r^{4}\\cos θ\\sin^{2} θdrdθ \\\\\n&= ∫_{3π/2}^{2π}3\\cos θ\\sin^{2} θ∫_{0}^{1}5r^{4}drdθ \\\\\n&= ∫_{3π/2}^{2π}3\\cos θ\\sin^{2} θ[r^{5}]_{0}^{1}dθ \\\\\n&= ∫_{3π/2}^{2π}3\\cos θ\\sin^{2} θ(1^{5} - 0)dθ \\\\\n&= ∫_{3π/2}^{2π}3\\cos θ\\sin^{2} θdθ \\\\\n&= [\\sin^{3} θ]_{3π/2}^{2π} \\\\\n&= (\\sin^{3} (2π) - \\sin^{3} (\\frac{3π}{2})) \\\\\n&= (0 - (-1)) \\\\\n&= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nf_{\\textrm{ave}} = \\dfrac{4}{\\pi} \\cdot 1 = \\dfrac{4}{\\pi}\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186163839 17592186163840 17592186163841 17592186163842 17592186163843 17592186163851 17592186163852 17592186163853 17592186163854 17592186163855] "immutable_fields" [[17592186163838 :answer-field/correct